Let the delete-on-exit toggle keep a list of sites, and clear everything else.

aerium-clear-browsing-data-on-exit.patch made the shutdown deletion a Settings
toggle with eight per-type checkboxes. This adds the other half of what people
actually ask for: a "never delete data for these sites" list, so signing back
into a handful of sites is not the price of a clean slate everywhere else.
It is the Cookie AutoDelete whitelist model - clear all sites except these -
rather than a second allowlist of sites to clear.

How the exclusion is applied
----------------------------
BrowsingDataRemover already supports this directly. content::
BrowsingDataFilterBuilder has a Mode::kPreserve, meaning "delete every origin
EXCEPT the ones added", and RemoveWithFilterAndReply() takes such a builder.
The exclusion list is turned into a kPreserve filter and the toggle's removal
goes through that instead of the unfiltered RemoveAndReply().

Entries are added with AddRegisterableDomain(). That method DCHECKs that what
it is handed is an eTLD+1, an IP address or an internal hostname - a bare
subdomain like www.example.com is rejected - so every entry is folded through
net::registry_controlled_domains::GetDomainAndRegistry() first, falling back
to the entry itself when that returns nothing (IP literals, single-label
hostnames such as "localhost"). This is the same normalisation
StartScheduledBrowsingDataRemoval() a few lines below already applies to the
URLs it protects, so the two protection mechanisms agree on what a "site" is.

Folding to eTLD+1 also fixes the granularity: listing example.com keeps
www.example.com and every other subdomain with it. That is what people mean
when they type a site into a "never clear these" box, and it is what Cookie
AutoDelete does.

Which data types the list can actually protect
----------------------------------------------
Not every data type can be deleted per origin, and getting this wrong is not
a cosmetic bug: ChromeBrowsingDataRemoverDelegate::RemoveEmbedderData() opens
with a CHECK - not a DCHECK, so it aborts release builds too - that the remove
mask is a subset of chrome_browsing_data_remover::FILTERABLE_DATA_TYPES
whenever the filter does not match all origins and domains. Handing it a
preserve filter together with, say, DATA_TYPE_HISTORY would crash the browser
on every shutdown.

FILTERABLE_DATA_TYPES at this revision is SITE_DATA | CACHE | DOWNLOADS |
RELATED_WEBSITE_SETS_PERMISSIONS | PREFETCH_CACHE | PRERENDER_CACHE. Of the
eight categories the toggle offers, that covers four:

  download history                 DATA_TYPE_DOWNLOADS        filterable
  cookies and other site data      DATA_TYPE_SITE_DATA        filterable
  cached images and files          DATA_TYPE_CACHE            filterable
  hosted app data                  DATA_TYPE_SITE_DATA        filterable

and leaves four that are not:

  browsing history                 DATA_TYPE_HISTORY          not filterable
  autofill form data               DATA_TYPE_FORM_DATA        not filterable
  saved passwords                  DATA_TYPE_PASSWORDS        not filterable
  site settings                    DATA_TYPE_CONTENT_SETTINGS not filterable

Those four are not filterable because their backends ignore the filter and
delete by time range only - the delegate is dotted with "TODO: Support all
backends with filter (crbug.com/40152186)" over exactly these code paths.
This is a real limitation of the browser at 151, not a shortcut taken here.

So the removal is split in two, the same way StartScheduledBrowsingDataRemoval()
below already splits its own mask on FILTERABLE_DATA_TYPES: the filterable
half goes through RemoveWithFilterAndReply() with the preserve filter, and the
unfilterable half goes through the ordinary RemoveAndReply() and is deleted
for every site.

Deleting the unfilterable half wholly, rather than skipping it whenever the
list is non-empty, is the deliberate choice. A checkbox the user ticked has to
delete what its label says; silently turning "delete my browsing history" into
"delete none of it, because you excluded one site" would be the worse
surprise, and it would leave history behind that the user believes is gone.
The Settings UI says plainly which four categories the list cannot protect
rather than leaving it to be discovered.

Scope: user toggle only
-----------------------
The exclusion list is applied to the Settings toggle's mask alone. The
enterprise ClearBrowsingDataOnExit policy and ungoogled-chromium's
#clear-data-on-exit flag keep their existing all-or-nothing semantics - a list
the user can edit must not be able to carve holes in managed configuration,
and the flag's whole point is that it wipes everything. When the list is
empty, which is the default, the code takes a single unfiltered
RemoveAndReply() that is byte for byte what it did before, so nothing changes
for profiles that never open the list.

The list-pref UI
----------------
One list pref, browser.clear_data.aerium_on_exit.excluded_sites. settingsPrivate
exposes list prefs as PrefType::kList, which neither settings-toggle-button nor
settings-checkbox can bind to - the trap the header of
aerium-clear-browsing-data-on-exit.patch describes, and the reason the eight
per-type prefs are booleans rather than a list.

Nothing is hand-rolled here to work around that. PrefsMixin already carries
appendPrefListItem()/deletePrefListItem() for precisely this case, and Chromium
already ships a site-exception list bound to a list pref -
performance_page/tab_discard/, the "always keep these sites active" list on the
Performance page. The new settings-clear-on-exit-sites element follows that
shape: a cr-row header with an Add button, a list-frame of rows with a clear
button each, and a cr-dialog with a cr-input for adding one. It composes
PrefsMixin, and privacy_page.ts already composed PrefsMixin too, so the
writable controls work.

Input handling is deliberately forgiving: a pasted URL, a bare hostname and a
content-settings style "[*.]example.com" pattern all resolve to the same
hostname, via the URL parser rather than a hand-written regex, so IDN, IPv6
literals and ports behave the way they do everywhere else in the browser.
Duplicates are rejected with a message instead of silently doing nothing.

The pref is added to the settingsPrivate allowlist in prefs_util.cc as
PrefType::kList; PrefsUtil::SetPref() handles base::Value::Type::LIST, so the
round trip works. A pref missing from that allowlist is refused with no error
at all and the control just does nothing.

Defaults are unchanged: the list starts empty, and passwords, site settings
and hosted app data are still off.
